  "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the digits $X$ and $Y$ such that the condition $\\overline{YXXX}_b = 5b^2 + 2b + 6$ holds true. The number $\\overline{YXXX}_b$ can be expressed as $Y \\cdot b^3 + X \\cdot b^2 + X \\cdot b + X \\cdot 1 = Yb^3 + X(b^2 + b + 1)$. Setting this equal to $5b^2 + 2b + 6$, we get the equation:\n\n\\[ Yb^3 + X(b^2 + b + 1) = 5b^2 + 2b + 6 \\]\n\nSince this equation must hold for all $b>5$, the coefficients of all powers of $b$ on both sides must match. Let's consider the equation for the lowest power of $b$ (i.e., $b^0$):\n\n\\[ X = 6 \\]\n\nWe can verify this solution by substituting $X = 6$ back into the equation:\n\n\\[ Yb^3 + 6(b^2 + b + 1) = 5b^2 + 2b + 6 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[ Yb^3 + 6b^2 + 6b + 6 \\]\n\nFor this to be equal to $5b^2 + 2b + 6$, the coefficients of the powers of $b$ must match. Thus, we get:\n\n\\[ Yb^3 + 6b^2 = 5b^2 \\]\n\nFrom this, we see that $Yb^3 = 0$. Since $b > 5$, the only solution for $Y$ that makes this equation true for all $b>5$ is $Y = 0$. Thus, we have:\n\n\\[ X = 6 \\]\n\\[ Y = 0 \\]\n\nGiven that $X > Y$ is satisfied, we can find $X - Y$:\n\n\\[ X - Y = 6 - 0 = 6 \\]\n\nSo the answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{6}\\]",
